# Connecting a host

Every MCP host starts a stdio server from a command and optional environment
variables. Build first (`make build`) and use the binary's absolute path:
hosts start servers from their own working directory.

Claude Code:

```sh
claude mcp add {{project_slug}} -- /absolute/path/to/bin/{{project_slug}}
claude mcp add --env {{env_prefix}}_LOG_LEVEL=debug {{project_slug}} -- /absolute/path/to/bin/{{project_slug}}
```

Options go before the server name; everything after `--` is the command.

Hosts configured with JSON (Claude Desktop, Cursor and others) take an entry
shaped like this under their MCP servers key; check the host's documentation
for the file and key name:

```json
{
  "{{project_slug}}": {
    "command": "/absolute/path/to/bin/{{project_slug}}",
    "args": [],
    "env": { "{{env_prefix}}_LOG_LEVEL": "info" }
  }
}
```

After a rebuild, restart the server from the host (or the host itself) so it
starts the new binary. If the host reports the server as failed, read the
host's MCP log: the server's stderr (JSON logs and startup errors) ends up
there.

Once the binary is released (GoReleaser, if added), point the command at the
installed binary instead of the checkout.
